{
  "image_name": "0ad1b914f8b10494e12412ab7dfa6148.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how double-positive (DP) thymocytes in the thymus are selected into different T-cell lineages based on the self-ligand they recognize and, on the right, the apparent strength of self-reactivity. In the left schematic, DP thymocytes interact with cortical thymic epithelial cells presenting either MHC-II or MHC-I. Recognition of MHC-II leads to a CD4 single-positive thymocyte, whereas recognition of MHC-I produces a CD8 single-positive thymocyte. These fates are linked to the opposing transcription factors ThPOK/ZBTB7B and RUNX3, shown as mutually antagonistic regulators. After activation in lymph nodes, the CD4 lineage gives rise to helper T cells and the CD8 lineage to killer T cells.\n\nThe right side expands this into a broader developmental map. From DP thymocytes, conventional CD8 and CD4 single-positive cells arise from peptide–MHC-I and peptide–MHC-II recognition and are grouped as “soldiers,” emphasizing defense against pathogens. Additional lineages—Treg cells, MAIT cells, IELs, and iNKT cells—branch from selection on peptide–MHC-II, MR1, peptide–MHC-I, or CD1d, respectively. These are grouped as “peacekeepers,” associated with tissue and immune homeostasis. A color gradient indicates increasing TCR affinity for self, with the nonconventional lineages appearing to be selected at relatively higher self-reactivity.",
